\fs20\b ILLUSTRATIVE EXAMPLE \'96 NOT LEGAL ADVICE.\b0\par
A working checklist, not a court form. Use the official application notice and route through your legal services. Source: Thinking About Policing.\par
\par
\fs28\b CIVIL INJUNCTION \'96 APPLICATION CHECKLIST\b0\fs20\par
\i Anti-social Behaviour, Crime and Policing Act 2014, Part 1 (sections 1\'9621)\i0\par
\par
\b The test\b0\par
\tab [ ] On the balance of probabilities, the respondent has engaged or threatens to engage in anti-social behaviour, meaning:\par
\tab\tab \'95 conduct that has caused, or is likely to cause, harassment, alarm or distress (non-housing context); or\par
\tab\tab \'95 conduct capable of causing nuisance or annoyance in a housing context.\par
\tab [ ] It is just and convenient to grant the injunction to prevent that behaviour.\par
\par
\b Evidence to assemble\b0\par
\tab [ ] Witness statements (including professional witnesses; hearsay is admissible).\par
\tab [ ] Incident logs, CAD records, body-worn footage, prior warnings/ABCs/CPNs.\par
\tab [ ] Community impact statement.\par
\par
\b Terms sought\b0\par
\tab [ ] Prohibitions (clear, proportionate) and/or positive requirements.\par
\tab [ ] Name the person/organisation supervising any requirement.\par
\par
\b Power of arrest\b0\par
\tab [ ] Seek a power of arrest where the behaviour includes the use or threatened use of violence, or there is a significant risk of harm.\par
\par
\b Process\b0\par
\tab [ ] Correct court: youth court (under 18); county court or High Court (adults).\par
\tab [ ] Under 18: consult the youth offending team.\par
\tab [ ] Consider an interim injunction (with or, if urgent, without notice).\par
\tab [ ] Service, hearing date, and a plan for monitoring and enforcing breach (contempt of court).\par
